m":"lexicon"},{"he":"האוטובוס","en":"the bus","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הבא","en":"next","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"רוכבים","en":"ride","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"סוסים","en":"horses","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"החופשה","en":"the holiday","alt":["the vacation"],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הראשונה","en":"the first","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"רציף","en":"platform","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"שלוש","en":"three (fem.)","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"יוצאת","en":"depart","alt":["is departing","is going out"],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"הטיסה","en":"the flight","alt":[],"from":"lexicon"},{"he":"ביקור","en":"visit","alt":["business card"],"from":"skill"},{"he":"סליחה","en":"excuse me","alt":["sorry","I am sorry"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"אנחנו","en":"we are","alt":["we"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"על","en":"on","alt":["over","about"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"זאת","en":"this","alt":["this is (feminine)","that is (feminine)"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"שלה","en":"her","alt":["hers","its"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"זה","en":"this is","alt":["this","it is"],"from":"phrase"},{"he":"שלךָ","en":"your (sg.masc.)","alt":["yours (sg.masc.)"],"from":"phrase"}],"lexemes":["הרכבת התחתית","רוכבים","סירה","הסירה","יבשת","רוכבת","היבשת","המדריכה","אמריקה","מפליגים","מפליגה","באמריקה","נוהג","תחבורה","דרכון","תחנת האוטובוס","מטיילות","באסיה","שטים","דלק","קו","האופנוע","הרכב","קווי","הטיסה","טסים","תחנת רכבת","אופנוע","למסע","הטיול","באנגליה","לטיול","המסע","ביקור","לאנגליה","מסע","לחופשה","הביקור","אנגליה","תיק הגב","החופשה","טיול","מכונית","הרכבת"],"tips":"**Gender of Countries**\n\nIf in doubt, remember that most countries are feminine in gender, since מדינה (country) and ארץ (land) are feminine words. \n\n**Abbreviations**\n\nIn this skill we come across חו\"ל. Abbreviations are very popular in Hebrew, and this is an abbreviation of חוץ לארץ, literally meaning \"outside of the land\", but with the meaning \"abroad\". (הארץ is another name for Israel, since Israel is sometimes known as ארץ ישראל - the land of Israel. Note that in its normal form, it is pronounced árets, and in its construct form, it is érets.)\n\nIn Hebrew abbreviations, some representative letters are chosen, often depending on what will make the mostly easily pronounceable word, so the letters chosen are not necessarily just the first letter of each word. The gershayim (technically ״, but since on most keyboards it is much easier to type the double punctuation marks: \") are always placed just before the last letter.\n\nLike in English, not all abbreviations are pronounced as words. \n\nNASA, for example, **is** pronounced \"na-sa\": נאס\"א.  \nBut the USA, ארה\"ב is pronounced the same way as ארצות הברית: artsót habrít.  \nUnlike in English, it is uncommon to spell out abbreviations (like USA: yu-ess-aye)."}